  "projectUrl": "https://github.com/NikolayIT/RestrictedProcess.NET",
  "published": "2026-08-25T15:14:55.517Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Version 2.0 rebuilds the sandbox on the current Windows security primitives. BREAKING: targets net8.0-windows and net10.0-windows (netstandard2.0 is dropped, so .NET Framework hosts are no longer supported); IExecutor is async-first and takes an ExecutionRequest carrying separate processor-time and wall-clock limits; limits are 64-bit. Security: every group in the token becomes deny-only except a per-level exception list, each run gets a unique SID used both as a restricting SID and as the only non-user entry in a locked-down default DACL, and the new WriteRestricted, StrictlyRestricted and Lockdown token levels join Limited and Restricted. The throwaway desktop is built with a least-privilege DACL and a mandatory label derived from the configured integrity level. The job object is attached at process creation, 128-bit process mitigation policies are supported, shell-brokered breakaway is blocked, and optional AppContainer/LPAC network blocking no longer leaves a permanent ACL grant on the executable. Measurement: job accounting and I/O completion port notifications replace polling, processor time covers the whole process tree including children that already exited, and memory defaults to peak committed bytes. Fixes: CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB is gone (it made process creation fail inside any job that forbids breakaway, including CI runners); command lines are escaped to CommandLineToArgvW rules; exit code 259 is no longer mistaken for a running process; output is drained to end of file rather than abandoned after 100 ms; and a run stopped at its time limit is no longer reported as a runtime error just because the program wrote to standard error. Diagnostics: SandboxException names the step that failed, and SandboxCapabilities.Probe() reports what the host supports.",
